WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.581
Step 4: count, then decide what to do.

2
00:00:02.861 --> 00:00:08.856
With a taxonomy, counting is mechanical: for each failure mode, how many failing traces show it.

3
00:00:09.136 --> 00:00:14.661
Count traces, not labels, so a note that mentions the same mode twice doesn’t double-count.

4
00:00:14.941 --> 00:00:25.544
Divide by the number of traces reviewed to get a rate, and keep the denominator visible; “9 of 36” is more honest than “25 percent” on a sample this small.

5
00:00:25.824 --> 00:00:27.979
Frequency alone isn’t priority.

6
00:00:28.259 --> 00:00:35.064
An invented refund policy hurts more than an over-polite reply, even if the polite reply is more common.

7
00:00:35.344 --> 00:00:45.349
A simple, transparent way to rank is to give each mode a severity (say 1 for cosmetic, 3 for harmful or costly) and sort by rate times severity.

8
00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:48.979
The weights are a judgment, so write down who set them and why.

9
00:00:49.259 --> 00:00:59.541
The FAQ suggests something similar when combining checks: report release-blocking failures separately from tolerable ones, rather than averaging everything together.

10
00:00:59.821 --> 00:01:01.677
Then decide, mode by mode.

11
00:01:01.957 --> 00:01:10.533
Some failures have an obvious, cheap fix (a missing rule in the prompt, a retrieval filter) that you can make and verify on the same traces.

12
00:01:10.813 --> 00:01:18.344
Others recur after fixes or need judgment to detect; those deserve an automated eval so you can track them over time.

13
00:01:18.624 --> 00:01:28.245
Next week you’ll build graders for exactly those modes, and Husain’s guide to LLM judges explains how to check a judge against the expert’s labels.
